* t50movesr.a -- CONF68K test t50: MOVE from SR completes in user state.** Claim (DOCS/claims.md): MOVE SR,<ea> is not a privileged instruction on* the MC68000 and MC68008. Motorola's M68000 Family Programmer's Reference* Manual lists it among the integer instructions for those two parts, and* among the supervisor instructions only from the MC68010 on. A user-state* program built for the 68000 may therefore read SR, and compilers of the* day did exactly that -- the RTF Fortran run-time reads SR to get at the* condition codes -- so a system that traps it kills every such program at* an ordinary-looking instruction.** What is measured is that the instruction COMPLETES. A system that raises* a privilege violation here kills this process before it prints anything,* so the evidence of a divergence is a MISSING result line, not a FAIL: the* tally comes up one short and tools/conformance.sh says which test. The* observed value is the S bit of the word the instruction delivered. A* program in user state must see it clear, which is what a 68000 shows.** On a 68010 or later the instruction IS privileged in silicon, and whether* the process survives is then the kernel's doing: Motorola's advice to* operating systems was to emulate it in the privilege-violation handler so* that 68000 binaries keep running. A report from a real 68020 OS-9 system* therefore answers a question nothing here can settle.*Prgrm set 1Objct set 1ReEnt set $80Type_Lang set (Prgrm<<8)+ObjctAttr_Rev set (ReEnt<<8)+1*SBIT set $2000*EXPECT set 0* psect t50movesr,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,0,2048,startstart moveq #0,d6 move sr,d6 and.l #SBIT,d6 cmpi.l #EXPECT,d6 beq.s ispass bra.s isfail* use verdict.i use report.i*idstr dc.b "RESULT t50 ",0passtr dc.b "PASS",0failstr dc.b "FAIL",0errstr dc.b "ERROR",0obsstr dc.b "  obs=",0expstr dc.b " exp=",0descstr dc.b "  MOVE from SR completes in user state with S clear",0 ends